Since $a_0 = \frac{1}{2}$ is less than $\lambda_1,$ $a_0 - a_1 > 0$ after the first step.  Since $a_1$ is less than $\lambda_1,$ $a_1 - a_2 > 0$ after the second step.  Thus, the product $a_0 a_1 a_2 a_3 \dotsb$ will approach 4 if $\theta^3 (a)$ eventually approaches $\lambda_1.$

It remains to analyze when $\theta^k (a)$ eventually approaches $\lambda_1,$ as $k$ becomes large.

Note that $0 < \lambda_2^3 < 1.$  Thus,
